libdssl -- native DSSL for FreeBWS, step 1: DSSL to CSS in C
============================================================

DSSL (huggingface.co/EHS-Studio/dssl, MIT) is a style language, an alternative to
CSS. Today it runs as a JavaScript engine that writes inline styles. Version 0.16.3
added native/spec: a written specification for implementations that do not need
JavaScript. libdssl is a small C11 library (libc and libm only) plus a command line
tool, dssl2css, that follows chapters 2 to 7 and 9 to 11 of that spec:

    DSSL text --> parser (tree) --> resolver (rule objects) --+--> JSON of the resolved document
                                                              +--> CSS (INT-6 lowering)
                                                              +--> list of what needs the engine

It is a proof of concept for a browser: FreeBWS can hand it a stylesheet and get CSS
back, and it can ask whether a sheet is something CSS can express at all. What CSS
cannot express (spec 11.4) is detected and listed, never half done: for such a sheet
FreeBWS keeps using the JavaScript engine it already bundles.

Based on: EHS-Studio/dssl, commit 0d7ad1f69d35d45f2be5fac16fb3b4e2b29350cf (v0.16.3,
"v0.16.3 binaries"); the spec itself arrived in 9da4a9b. The reference behaviour is the
engine's public/dssl/parser.js, resolver.js and static.js of that commit, where the spec
leaves room (SPEC-NOTES.md lists every place where it does and where the two disagree).
License: MIT, like DSSL. The word lists in vocab.c come from the DSSL repo's
native/data/vocabulary.json (tools/genvocab.mjs).


Status (measured on this tree)
------------------------------

    make check
      level 1  (syntax tree + parser errors)     700 / 700 passed
      level 2  (resolved document, canonical JSON) 636 / 636 passed, 64 skipped (aura, slider and
                                                    texture-with-errors: the JS engine owns those values)
      CSS lane (byte-equal to the engine's `dssl build` output, where the sheet lowers completely)
                                                 552 / 552 passed, 23 skipped (textures are baked into
                                                    images by the engine's lane; libdssl leaves them out)
      multi-sheet (token and motion registries flow forward)   3 / 3 passed
      golden (lowering of animate:, stack:, seen, fonts, raw, several sheets, feature list)  5 / 5
      fuzz: every corpus input cut at random points and mutated, plus adversarial shapes: no crash
    make asan        the same under AddressSanitizer and UBSan: clean
    make valgrind    clean, no leaks (the library allocates from one arena per sheet and context)
    make bench       parse + resolve of 100 kB of DSSL: 4.8 ms (best of 40, -O2, one core of this
                     laptop); lowering to CSS another 0.8 ms; peak memory about 4 MB for 66 kB

"Passed" means byte-identical canonical JSON (spec 10.3) against what the reference engine
produces for the same input, including every error and warning with its line and wording.
That is a strong claim, so read this before trusting it:

  * The 0.16.3 checkout does not contain the conformance corpus that spec chapter 10 describes
    (native/expected/, scripts/native-dump.mjs, native/corpus/EXCLUDED.md, native/tests/):
    only the prose. The corpus here is therefore ours (corpus/, 703 inputs): the 9 stylesheets
    the project ships, all 84 fenced ```dssl blocks of its guide, readme, usage notes and spec,
    526 string literals out of its own test files that look like DSSL (fixtures), and 81 cases
    written for this library, one or more per feature and per diagnostic message (tools/handcases.txt).
  * The expected output (expected/) was produced by running the project's own engine files over
    those inputs with tools/dump.mjs, in the canonical form of spec 10.3. Whatever the engine did
    is "right", including its bugs; SPEC-NOTES.md lists the ones that matter. Where the engine
    contradicts the spec, we follow the engine, because the rule objects are compared against it.
  * Lint warnings are not produced (DIA-12 allows that); they are filtered from the expected files.
  * 64 + 23 inputs are skipped, listed by name in the test output, with the reason. Nothing is
    silently excluded, there is no allow-list of failures (expected/KNOWN-FAILURES may be used
    for one; it does not exist because nothing fails).
  * Coverage of the C code by conformance, golden and fuzz runs together is 95 to 100 percent of the
    lines of each source file (gcov). What they do not reach is mostly out-of-memory handling.


What is implemented
-------------------

Parser (spec 2, level 1): comments (// and /* */, quote- and url()-aware, line numbers kept),
logical lines (parentheses, trailing comma or colon), chunks, header classification, one-line
blocks, declarations (`use`, bare words, numeric token keys, token pairs on one line), raw { }
and fonts { } capture (single and multi line), unknown at-rules skipped whole, error recovery
with the same recovery as the reference.

Resolver (spec 3 to 7, 9, 10, level 2):
  values       bare numbers get px, ms for time properties; tokens become var(--dssl-...); alpha
               (color.ink/20, #f00/50, white/12); keywords, bareIdents, functions (repeat,
               url, counter...), escaped quotes; unknown identifiers with did-you-mean hints
  tokens       blocks and nesting, numeric keys, conditional tokens, scoped (--color.accent),
               registry rule, the registry that flows from sheet to sheet
  cascade      layers (tokens, base, components, utilities, pin), nesting with & and selector
               lists, states, order numbers and fragments, @media and @container sugar
               (768, <= 480, 480..960, dark, light, reduced-motion, bare numbers in comparisons),
               pseudo-elements as shadow rules (nested and written in the selector),
               behaviors and use (cycles, duplicates), @signal conditions (parsed and recorded)
  properties   size, row, column, grid, stack, place, center, align, circle, cover, sticky,
               z, grow, shrink, basis, truncate, visually-hidden, font, fluid (and font-size:
               fluid 16 24 on any length property), shadow, radius, transition, gradient and
               gradient-text (all kinds, mesh, tiles, offsets, stops, midpoints, colour spaces),
               text-stroke, outline-shape, rotate, glass, yield, and the records of animate,
               stagger, overlap, bend, bend-y, bend-up, fold, bend-around, stretch,
               gradient-flow and tilt
  motions      motion blocks to frames (deform words composed into transform), linked to
               animate:, shared between sheets
  diagnostics  every message of the reference with its hint; errors carry line and column;
               duplicates removed as in DIA-5; warnings (state-word typos, row/column word
               clashes, display clashes, raw semicolons, background image twice, animation
               longhands, unknown layers, radius tokens, animation names)
CSS lowering (spec 11.3, INT-6): a port of the engine's static lane (toCSS): `@layer tokens,
base, components, utilities, pin;`, :where() around every selector so order decides, pin as
!important, @media and @container wrappers, @keyframes, raw verbatim, fonts as @import and
@font-face, tokens as custom properties. Output is byte-equal to the engine's for every
corpus sheet that lowers completely.

Additions that the engine's static lane does not have (marked "soft" in the API):
  animate:   a CSS animation in the same rule; non-essential motions only under
             (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference); `jitter` is not lowered (hard).
  stack:     the rule gets display: grid and place-items as before, plus a child rule
             `:where(sel > *) { grid-area: 1 / 1 }` (what the engine does with inline styles).
  seen:      applied unconditionally, as the engine's lane does (but still reported: hard).
  CAS-47     alignment words without a display (`center:` on a span): CSS cannot promote the box,
             reported as soft "align-promotion" so a page can decide.


What falls back to the JavaScript engine
----------------------------------------

dssl_needs_engine(sheet) returns a bit mask of the hard features; dssl_feature_at() lists each
occurrence with line and column. A browser should use the JS engine for the whole page when any
sheet of the page has a hard feature (see "Integration" for why not per sheet):

    DSSL_FEAT_SIGNAL         @signal blocks (the page sets named values; needs a store)
    DSSL_FEAT_SEEN           seen { } / :seen (first time in the viewport)
    DSSL_FEAT_ANIMATE_JITTER animate: ... jitter N
    DSSL_FEAT_STAGGER        stagger:
    DSSL_FEAT_TEXTURE        texture: (the values are not computed; the JSON says "<engine>")
    DSSL_FEAT_AURA           aura, aura-glow, aura-particle, glow-particle... (same)
    DSSL_FEAT_DEFORM         bend, bend-y, bend-up, fold, stretch, bend-around (need box sizes)
    DSSL_FEAT_TILT           tilt: (pointer driven)
    DSSL_FEAT_SLIDER         slider: (values not computed, vendor parts not generated)
    DSSL_FEAT_OVERLAP        overlap: (a hook after layout; not even specified yet)
    DSSL_FEAT_GRADIENT_FLOW  gradient-flow: (loops background-position through the Web
                             Animations API)
  soft (lowered approximately, never block native use): DSSL_FEAT_ANIMATE, DSSL_FEAT_STACK,
  DSSL_FEAT_ALIGN_PROMOTION.

Everything else of the spec's "part that lowers to CSS" is native.

Not done on purpose or not yet: the lint pass (contrast, focus outline, small targets; spec
DIA-12 allows leaving it out), the paint half of texture/aura/slider (spec chapter 8 marks it
Pending), @slider's base declarations and vendor shadow rules, Unicode white space other than
ASCII (the engine's regexes treat NBSP, U+2028 and friends as white space, we do not),
element matching and computed styles (level 3, that is what WebKit does with the CSS).


Differences from the JavaScript engine (all deliberate)
-------------------------------------------------------

  * @keyframes keep vendor-prefixed property names (-webkit-text-fill-color). The engine's static
    lane loses the leading dash when it turns camelCase back into kebab-case. The rule objects
    (JSON) still use camelCase as the spec says.
  * Property names that are members of JavaScript's Object.prototype (constructor, toString,
    __proto__...) as property, behavior, motion or place names: the engine reads them from plain
    objects (garbage declarations, or a TypeError that kills the sheet); here they are ordinary
    unknown names.
  * Limits: block nesting beyond 400 levels is skipped with an error; behavior expansion is
    budgeted (6 million steps); memory per sheet is capped at 48 MB + 64 x the input size
    (dssl_parse returns NULL beyond that); input over 32 MiB is refused.
  * Invalid UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD; a leading byte order mark is dropped (as TextDecoder does).
  * Diagnostics carry a column (the engine has none). The line is the spec's (SYN-2: the line
    where the chunk started); the column is where the construct starts on its own physical line,
    so for a selector list spread over several lines the two refer to different lines.
  * The CSS lane's registry rule: the first sheet of a context gets every token; later sheets
    only the tokens that are new to the context, so a later sheet cannot override an earlier
    sheet's conditional value with an unconditional one (spec D-6, open in the spec).


API (dssl.h)
------------

    dssl_ctx *ctx = dssl_ctx_new();                 /* one per document */
    dssl_sheet *s = dssl_parse(text, len, url, ctx);/* in load order; tokens and motions flow on */
    unsigned hard = dssl_needs_engine(s);           /* 0: the CSS below is complete */
    char *css = dssl_to_css(s, 0);                  /* free() it; DSSL_CSS_BANNER adds a comment */
    char *json = dssl_to_json(s);                   /* the spec's rule objects, canonical JSON */
    dssl_diag d; for (i = 0; dssl_error_at(s, i, &d); i++) ...   /* file:line:col, message, hint */
    dssl_feature f; for (i = 0; dssl_feature_at(s, i, &f); i++) ... /* what needs the engine, where */
    dssl_free(s); dssl_ctx_free(ctx);

dssl_parse(NULL ctx) works for a single sheet. A sheet must not be used after its context is
freed. Nothing is global, so sheets of different pages can be parsed on different threads. Safe
on any input: arbitrary bytes, truncated files, hostile nesting; on out of memory or past the
limits dssl_parse returns NULL and nothing leaks.

Command line:
    dssl2css site.dssl > site.css           files are one cascade, in the order given
    dssl2css --json a.dssl b.dssl           rule objects, one line per file
    dssl2css --features page.dssl           what needs the engine, with line:col
    dssl2css --check --strict-engine *.dssl exit 1 on errors, 3 when a sheet needs the engine
    dssl2css --tree x.dssl                  syntax tree and parser errors as JSON (level 1)


Integration into FreeBWS (implemented in src/freebws, see docs/M8-BROWSER.md)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

FreeBWS already serves the JS engine to pages that carry DSSL tags: a user script (end of
document) looks for <link rel=stylesheet type=text/dssl>, <style type=text/dssl> and friends
and imports freebws-dssl://engine/boot.js (docs/M8-BROWSER.md). The native path slots in front:

1. Collect. The same user script, instead of importing the engine at once, gathers every DSSL
   source of the page in document order: the text of <style type=text/dssl>, and for each
   <link type=text/dssl href> the text, fetched in the page (fetch(), same rules the engine
   follows today, so cross-origin and file:// behave as now). It posts them in one message:
       window.webkit.messageHandlers.dssl.postMessage({sheets:[{url, text, nodeIndex}, ...]})
   Native side: webkit_user_content_manager_register_script_message_handler(ucm, "dssl")
   and the "script-message-received::dssl" signal. A custom URI scheme handler is the
   alternative for <link>s (rewrite href to freebws-dsslcss://?u=...; native fetches with libsoup
   and answers with text/css): no flash, no page-side fetch, but the scheme is subject to the
   page's style-src CSP and the fetch bypasses the page's CORS decision, so it is the second choice.
2. Decide. In the handler: one dssl_ctx per top-level page (drop it on navigation), dssl_parse()
   every sheet in order. If any sheet has dssl_needs_engine() != 0, or the page ships its own
   engine (script[src*=dssl]) or uses the DSSL JS API, answer {native:false} and the script
   imports the engine exactly as it does today. The decision is per page, not per sheet:
   the engine writes inline styles, which beat any stylesheet, while lowered CSS lives in
   cascade layers; mixing the two would flip who wins between DSSL rules of different sheets.
3. Apply. Otherwise native answers {native:true, css:["...", ...]} (dssl_to_css() per sheet) and
   the script inserts one <style data-dssl-native> per source at the position of the original
   tag, so load order is the order of the sheets. Inline <style> elements are subject to the page's
   style-src; where that blocks it (strict CSP), the same CSS can go in through
   webkit_user_content_manager_add_style_sheet() (not subject to CSP; to be tried: it must be
   removed on navigation and its cascade position against the page's own sheets checked), or
   the page falls back to the JS engine, which has the same CSP problem as today.
4. Later changes. A MutationObserver for DSSL tags added after load re-collects the page and
   repeats from step 2 (and takes the engine path if that is needed).
5. Diagnostics. dssl_error_at()/dssl_warning_at() go to the WebKit console (webkit_..._console
   message from the handler, or an inspector message) in the engine's format, and `dssl2css --check`
   gives the same on the command line.

Things to settle before it replaces the JS path (each is a line in SPEC-NOTES.md): where lowered
DSSL layers sit relative to unlayered page CSS (every normal stylesheet beats a cascade layer,
the JS engine's inline styles beat the page's CSS, so `yield:` and conflicts between DSSL and CSS
change meaning); raw { } is emitted unlayered and therefore beats every DSSL rule except pin;
page scripts that read computed styles before the sheet is applied.


Rebuilding the corpus (needs node, runs the reference engine files; read them first)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    git clone https://huggingface.co/EHS-Studio/dssl REPO        # commit 0d7ad1f6...
    node --permission --allow-fs-read=REPO --allow-fs-read=$PWD/tools --allow-fs-write=$PWD/corpus \
         tools/mkcorpus.mjs REPO $PWD/corpus tools/handcases.txt
    node --permission --allow-fs-read=REPO/public/dssl --allow-fs-read=$PWD/tools \
         --allow-fs-read=$PWD/corpus --allow-fs-write=$PWD/expected \
         tools/dump.mjs REPO/public/dssl $PWD/corpus $PWD/expected
    node --permission --allow-fs-read=REPO/native/data/vocabulary.json --allow-fs-read=$PWD/tools \
         --allow-fs-write=$PWD tools/genvocab.mjs REPO/native/data/vocabulary.json $PWD

Files
-----
    dssl.h  public API            internal.h  types shared by the .c files
    util.c  arena, strings, JS number formatting, did-you-mean
    parse.c syntax (chapter 2)    value.c  values (3) and the token map
    props.c semantic properties   gradient.c  gradient: and gradient-text:
    resolve.c  resolver, motions, fonts, public API
    emit_json.c  canonical JSON   emit_css.c  lowering
    vocab.c/.h  generated word lists (the engine's order matters for did-you-mean ties)
    main.c  dssl2css              tests/conform.c  conformance, fuzz and bench runner
    tests/golden/  reviewed output for what the reference lane has no word for
    corpus/ expected/  inputs and expected output (MIT, derived from the DSSL repo)
    tools/  mkcorpus.mjs dump.mjs genvocab.mjs handcases.txt (not used by the build)
    SPEC-NOTES.md  ambiguities and bugs found in the spec, for the DSSL developers
